title = Branch policies, reviewers, and merge types in Azure Repos
mode = platform_theory

[diagram]

[code]
   branch: main (protected)
        │
        ├── require PR (no direct pushes)
        ├── min reviewers + optional path owners
        ├── build validation (pipeline must pass)
        ├── linked work items (traceability)
        └── merge types allowed (basic / squash / rebase+FF)
[/code]

[center][color=#0078d4]policy overrides[/color]  ·  inherit from project or set per branch[/center]

[code]
   direct push to main ──X──► policy blocks ref update
   merge via completed PR ──✓──► allowed strategies only
[/code]

[description]

[b]Branch policies[/b] in Azure Repos attach to branch name patterns (typically [code]main[/code] or [code]release/*[/code]) and enforce server-side rules before protected refs move—complementing the Git skills from Oh My Git where you learned that [code]git push --force[/code] rewrites history: here the server refuses if policy forbids force-push or direct commits.

[b]Require pull requests:[/b] blocks pushes that would advance the protected branch without an active, completed PR. Minimum reviewer counts, required reviewers, and “prohibit merge until last vote is approve” mirror human review gates. Optional automatic reviewers by path approximate CODEOWNERS—files under [code]/security/[/code] might always summon the security Team.

[b]Build validation:[/b] ties a specific Azure Pipeline YAML definition to policy: the PR must show a successful run from that pipeline before Complete. Misconfigured pipeline triggers ([code]pr:[/code] branches only) leave PRs red forever—match trigger paths to how teams branch ([code]feature/*[/code]).

[b]Work item and comment gates:[/b] require linked Boards items for traceability; require comment resolution so review threads cannot linger unresolved. Great for regulated delivery; painful if developers forget IDs—provide branch naming templates and CLI helpers ([code]az boards work-item show[/code]).

[b]Merge strategy policy:[/b] allow subset of basic merge, squash merge, rebase merge (fast-forward only). Squash produces one commit on [code]main[/code] like Oh My Git squash lessons; rebase+FF keeps linear history without merge nodes; basic merge preserves branch topology for archaeology.

[b]Bypass and override permissions:[/b] Project Collection Administrators or explicitly granted bypass roles can override policy—treat as audited break-glass, not daily workflow. Policy inheritance from project settings vs per-branch overrides confuses audits: document which pattern you use.

[b]Path filters and scope:[/b] optional policies can apply only when certain paths change—useful for [code]docs/**[/code] vs [code]src/**[/code] separation. Misconfigured globs either over-enforce (docs PRs require full QA pipeline) or under-enforce (security-sensitive paths merge without owners). Review glob diffs in policy change PRs the same way you review [code].gitattributes[/code]. Document exempt branches ([code]hotfix/*[/code]) in runbooks so on-call knows which policies still apply during incidents.

[b]When things go wrong:[/b] teams sometimes enable every gate at once and wonder why nothing merges—stack policies incrementally. “Build queued but never started” usually means no available agents in the pool the YAML targets, not failing tests. Emergency fixes blocked by work-item links need a documented hotfix path or temporary policy exception with post-merge backfill. Path filter typos silently skip required reviewers—test policy diffs against sample PR file lists.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat policies as executable documentation: if policy does not match how the team really ships, fix the process or simplify policy to avoid shadow workflows.
